From: baseband lane
Subject: 0540 pandora_mpss: two defects that made attach mode impossible, found by reading it

Both of these are in step_queues, and both only show up in the state attach mode exists for -- a
GLINK session that an earlier boot already created.

1. "Exists" was treated as failure.

   qcom_smem_alloc() returns QcomSmemExists when the item is already in the SMEM table, and it
   returns early without touching the payload (QcomSmem.c:97-101).  step_queues treated that
   non-zero code as an error and returned false, so step 9 failed whenever the GLINK descriptor and
   FIFO were already there -- which is precisely the attach case, and also why a second boot in the
   same session would fail at step 9.  Exists now means "kept", err is cleared, and the log says
   which of the two happened.

2. qcom_glink_init() zeroed the head and tail of a FIFO it did not create.

   qcom_glink_init() writes *tx.head = 0 and *rx.tail = 0 unconditionally.  When the descriptor is
   reused, d[0] is our write pointer and d[2] is the modem's write pointer into our FIFO, and the
   earlier session may well have moved both.  Clearing only those two leaves them disagreeing with
   the pointers at d[1] and d[3], so the link would look full or hand over stale bytes.  The values
   read at the top of the step are now put back when the descriptor was kept, and printed.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c b/b/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c
index 432e4fc..3d2eda8 100644
--- a/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c
@@ -1420,20 +1420,49 @@ step_queues(void)
 		printf("PANDORA MPSS: GLINK descriptor from an earlier session: our FIFO tail %u head %u, theirs tail %u head "
 		    "%u\n", was[0], was[1], was[2], was[3]);
 	}
+	/*
+	 * Exists means attach, not failure.
+	 *
+	 * qcom_smem_alloc() returns QcomSmemExists when the item is already in the table, and it does
+	 * not touch the payload in that case (QcomSmem.c:97-101 returns early).  This step used to
+	 * treat that non-zero code as an error and bail out -- which made the whole attach path
+	 * unreachable, because "the items are already there" is exactly the state attach is built on,
+	 * and it also made a second boot in the same session fail at step 9 for the same reason.
+	 */
 	int err = mp_smem_alloc(MP_ITEM_GL_DESC, 32);
+	bool desc_kept = err == QcomSmemExists;
+	if (desc_kept) {
+		err = 0;
+	}
 	if (!err) {
 		err = mp_smem_alloc(MP_ITEM_GL_TX, MP_GL_FIFO);
+		if (err == QcomSmemExists) {
+			err = 0;
+		}
 	}
 	uint32_t tsize = 0;
 	volatile uint8_t *desc = mp_smem_item(MP_ITEM_GL_DESC, &dsize), *txf = mp_smem_item(MP_ITEM_GL_TX, &tsize);
-	printf("PANDORA MPSS: GLINK items %u (%u bytes) %u (%u bytes) -> %d\n", MP_ITEM_GL_DESC, dsize, MP_ITEM_GL_TX,
-	    tsize, err);
+	printf("PANDORA MPSS: GLINK items %u (%u bytes, %s) %u (%u bytes) -> %d\n", MP_ITEM_GL_DESC, dsize,
+	    desc_kept ? "kept from the earlier session" : "allocated now", MP_ITEM_GL_TX, tsize, err);
 	if (err || !desc || !txf || dsize < 32) {
 		return false;
 	}
 	volatile uint32_t *d = (volatile uint32_t *)desc;
 	QcomGlinkFifo tx = {&d[1], &d[0], txf, tsize}, rx = {&d[3], &d[2], NULL, 0};
 	qcom_glink_init(&mp_gl, tx, rx, mp_gl_kick, NULL);
+	/*
+	 * qcom_glink_init zeroes *tx.head and *rx.tail.  That is right when this boot is the one that
+	 * creates the FIFOs, and wrong when they are being reused: d[0] is our write pointer and d[2] is
+	 * the modem's write pointer into our FIFO, both of which the earlier session may well have moved.
+	 * Clearing them would make the modem's tail disagree with our head and the link would look full
+	 * or hand us stale bytes.  Put back exactly what was read above.
+	 */
+	if (desc_kept) {
+		d[0] = was[0];
+		d[2] = was[2];
+		printf("PANDORA MPSS: FIFO pointers kept: our head %u, their tail %u (their head %u)\n",
+		    was[0], was[2], was[3]);
+	}
 	mp_gl_on = true;
 	return true;
 }
